<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glyptothek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Munich Glyptothek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Glyptothek is a museum of ancient Greek and Roman sculpture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I of Bavaria</a> and built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-von-klenze">Leo von Klenze</a> between 1816 and 1830. It was the first public museum in Munich. Its collection includes the pediment sculptures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-aphaia">Temple of Aphaia</a> on Aegina and the Barberini Faun.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glyptothek.jpg" alt="Glyptothek" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: High Contrast · CC BY 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glyptothek_in_M%C3%BCnchen_in_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Crown Prince Ludwig of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, later Ludwig I, commissioned the Glyptothek as a monument to ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. He envisioned a German Athens in which Greek culture would be remembered, and had it built in front of the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. It stands beside the Königsplatz and the building that houses the State Collection of Greek and Roman Antiquities, and it is the oldest public museum in Munich. The architects Karl von Fischer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-von-klenze">Leo von Klenze</a> laid out the Königsplatz in 1815, and Klenze arranged it like a forum, with the Glyptothek on its north side.</p><p>Between 1806 and the opening in 1830 Ludwig built up a notable collection through his agents. It came to include the Medusa Rondanini, the Barberini Faun and, in 1813, the figures from the temple of Aphaea on Aegina. Frescoes and stuccos by Peter von Cornelius, Clemens von Zimmermann and Wilhelm von Kaulbach once adorned the walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The building is neoclassical. Twelve Ionic columns form the portico, and thirteen rooms are arranged around a courtyard. The gable carries a group by Johann Martin von Wagner showing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> as protector of the plastic arts.</p><p>The collection runs from the Archaic age, about 650 BCE, to the Roman era, about 550 CE. Archaic works include the Munich Kouros and the temple figures from Aegina. Classical pieces include the portrait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, the Medusa Rondanini and the Alexander Rondanini, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> is represented above all by the Barberini Faun. The Roman galleries hold busts of Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>. A bust of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> excavated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1937 was acquired in 2017, so the museum owns portraits of all five Julio-Claudian emperors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> did not destroy much of the sculpture, but the frescoes did not survive, and when the museum reopened in 1972 only lightly plastered bricks were visible. Klenze's Assyrian Hall of 1864 was not rebuilt, and the Assyrian reliefs from the palace of Ashur-nasir-pal II and a lion from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> went to the Staatliche Sammlung für Ägyptische Kunst. It is now part of the Kunstareal, and its sculptures are complemented by the terracotta and bronze collections of the Antikensammlung opposite.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Glyptothek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His architects Leo von Klenze and Friedrich von Gärtner laid out the Ludwigstrasse and built the <b>Glyptothek</b> for classical sculpture and the Alte Pinakothek, opened in 1836, one of the first large public picture galleries in Europe, while the Walhalla near Regensburg, completed in 1842, celebrated German national…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
